```

The CSS might look as follows. In normal flow the elements will scroll with the content. When we add `position: sticky` to the element, along with a value of 0, supporting browsers will stick the headings to the top of the viewport as they reach that position. Each subsequent header will then replace the previous one as it scrolls up to that position.

```css
dt {
 background-color: black;
 color: white;
 padding: 10px;
 position: sticky;
 top: 0;
 left: 0;
 margin: 1em 0;
}
```

```css hidden
