## Blend modes

CSS blend modes allow us to add blend modes to elements that specify a blending effect when two elements overlap — the final color shown for each pixel will be the result of a combination of the original pixel color, and that of the pixel in the layer underneath it. Blend modes are again very familiar to users of graphics applications like Photoshop.

There are two properties that use blend modes in CSS:

- , which blends together multiple background images and colors set on a single element.
- , which blends together the element it is set on with elements it is overlapping — both background and content.

You can find a lot more examples than are available here in our [blend-modes.html](https://mdn.github.io/learning-area/css/styling-boxes/advanced_box_effects/blend-modes.html) example page (see [source code](https://github.com/mdn/learning-area/blob/main/css/styling-boxes/advanced_box_effects/blend-modes.html)), and on the reference page.

> [!NOTE]
> Blend modes are also very new, and slightly less well supported than filters. There is no support as yet in Edge, and Safari only supports some of the blend mode options.

### background-blend-mode

Again, let's look at some examples so we can understand this better. First, — here we'll show a couple of simple s, so you can compare the original with the blended version:

```html


